From 751bd62c84d97f9754a48f5614bc8fb1facd80fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yinvoker <yinvoker@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 00:01:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] perf: NEON small-GEMM fast path for the attention float bdot
 (bit-exact vs ruy)

The per-step attention matmuls (QK^T with transB and AV, batchC = B x
heads, head dim 48) are dominated by ruy::Mul's fixed per-call cost
(TrMulParams, thread probe, FPCR write, allocator reset, pack) and by
the 8x8 float tile's padding when M == 1. ProdBatchedOld now routes
shapes with M == 1, or !transB && N <= 64, to a hand-written NEON
kernel (tensors/cpu/small_sgemm_neon.cpp) when !transA && beta == 0.
Everything else stays on ruy.

The kernel reproduces ruy's generic KernelFloatNeon accumulation order
(leading 4x4 quadrant ascending; the other quadrants defer depth
(K & ~3) - 4 to after W - 1 under MAX_STREAMING when K >= 8), one
accumulator per output, fma every step, alpha applied afterwards, so
results are bit-identical on cores where ruy picks the generic kernel:
host random sweep 2408 shapes / 0 mismatches; device/200 canonical
hashes unchanged on 8 Gen 1 and on 865 big cores, both arms. On A55
cores ruy uses its A55ish kernel (pure ascending order) and the two
paths already differ from the big-core hash, on or off.

Measured, same binary, interleaved, same frequency caps, mbw512:
8 Gen 1 w1 en->zh 1.14x, pivot 1.14x; 865 w1 1.076x, blocking 1.077x,
pivot 1.082x, A55-pinned w1 1.080x; host M3 (ruy float) 1.08x.
Microbench geomean 3.0x (8 Gen 1), 2.9x (865 A77).

BERGAMOT_NO_SMALLGEMM=1 disables the path for same-binary A/B.
Microbench and bit sweep: tools/float-bench (-DBUILD_FLOAT_BENCH=ON,
FLOAT_BENCH_SWEEP=1).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
